# Block npm lifecycle scripts (preinstall/install/postinstall) on every install
# in this workspace — the same guard `frontend/.npmrc` has carried since the
# supply-chain audit (#562a).
#
# 🔴 NO LONGER A NO-OP, as of the electron-builder 25 -> 26 bump (#759 Half 2).
# This file used to record that the tree had ZERO install scripts. v26 pulls
# `electron-builder-squirrel-windows` -> `electron-winstaller@5.4.0`, which
# declares `"install": "node ./script/select-7z-arch.js"` — so the flag now
# actually blocks something on every install here and in CI.
#
# Measured after the bump: the script does NOT run (electron-winstaller/vendor
# still holds the unselected multi-arch 7z binaries), `npm ci` exits 0, and all
# electron tests pass — because we build the `nsis` target and never Squirrel,
# so nothing ever loads the package. ⚠️ Adding a `squirrel` Windows target would
# therefore fail in a confusing, unrelated-looking way (no 7z selected for the
# arch); select it manually or lift this flag deliberately if that day comes.
#
# ⚠️ And correct the prediction this file used to make: a blocked install script
# does NOT "fail LOUDLY". It failed SILENTLY here — nothing in npm's output,
# exit codes or the test suite mentions it. Loudness depends entirely on whether
# the package is ever exercised. The only reason we know is that the bump review
# diffed `hasInstallScript` across the lockfiles, which is now the way to see it.
#
# Why the guard is kept regardless: the June-2026 phantom-gyp campaign (57
# packages executing a malicious binding.gyp through node-gyp at install time) is
# the shape this defends against, and the exposure arrives exactly like this one
# did — with a *dev*-dependency bump, on a machine holding `gh` tokens and
# signing config. npm v12 makes script-blocking the default; this front-runs it.
ignore-scripts=true
